          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the figures below.</strong> We have not tested or
          measured these machines. Belt lengths, widths and motor ratings are the manufacturers'
          published figures. Usable deck length — the flat surface between the front roller
          housing and the point where the rear roller becomes a hazard — is always shorter than
          the advertised belt length and is rarely published, so ask the retailer for it and
          verify against the exact model year you are buying.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#stride-arithmetic">The Stride Arithmetic Nobody Does</a></li>
            <li><a href="#usable-length">Advertised Length Versus Usable Length</a></li>
            <li><a href="#ceiling">Ceiling Height: The Constraint Nobody Checks</a></li>
            <li><a href="#console-height">Console and Handrail Height</a></li>
            <li><a href="#how-we-tested">How We Chose</a></li>
            <li><a href="#picks">The Six Best Treadmills for Tall Runners</a></li>
            <li><a href="#speed">Top Speed and Why It Rarely Matters</a></li>
            <li><a href="#delivery">Delivery, Assembly and the Room It Ends Up In</a></li>
            <li><a href="#sharing">Sharing a Machine With a Much Shorter Runner</a></li>
            <li><a href="#verdict">Final Recommendations</a></li>
